# Summary
Introduced: the commit removed the prior rejection of X-Tenant-Id for unscoped tokens and added a DB membership fallback that authorizes tenant access based on membership existence only, without checking the role stored in user_tenant_roles against the endpoint's required role.
The new tenant-membership fallback checks only whether the authenticated subject appears in app.user_tenant_roles for the requested tenant. It does not verify that the stored per-tenant role meets the endpoint's required role. Router-level require_min_role('admin') still checks only the JWT roles, which may be unscoped or from another/default tenant. As a result, a user with an unscoped token carrying an admin role, and only viewer/agent membership in a victim tenant, can pass ensure_tenant_access for that victim tenant and invoke tenant-admin endpoints such as PATCH /tenants/{tenant_id}/settings or POST /tenants/{tenant_id}/channels/whatsapp. Those endpoints then set app.tenant_id to the victim tenant and perform privileged updates.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the current commit accepts X-Tenant-Id with an unscoped non-support user token instead of rejecting it.
- [x] Confirm router-level admin RBAC checks only JWT roles and does not consult per-tenant user_tenant_roles.role.
- [x] Confirm the new tenant membership fallback checks only existence of any user_tenant_roles row for actor and tenant.
- [x] Confirm a tenant-admin endpoint calls ensure_tenant_access and then performs a privileged tenant-scoped write.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce the bypass with a low-privileged tenant membership and an unscoped admin JWT using actual application functions.
- [ ] Crash/Valgrind reproduction: not applicable to this Python authorization logic flaw; valgrind/gdb were not installed in the container and the vulnerability is not memory-safety related.
## Report
Validated the finding at commit e942cfd5f5d51a99eb94c6187392d1d8abf1ab36. Code review showed the introduced behavior: app/core/security.py:179-190 only rejects mismatched X-Tenant-Id when the token already has a tenant_id; an unscoped non-support token with X-Tenant-Id is accepted and leaves request.state.tenant_id=None while preserving requested_tenant_id. app/core/security.py:218-230 implements require_min_role using only request.state.roles from the JWT. app/api/v1/routes.py:56-72 implements has_user_tenant_role as select exists(...) over app.user_tenant_roles without selecting or filtering utr.role, and app/api/v1/routes.py:76-85 allows access when that existence check returns true. app/api/v1/routes.py:259-285 then uses ensure_tenant_access and performs the tenant settings update after setting app.tenant_id. I reproduced this with tenant_rbac_bypass_poc.py using the real authenticate_request, require_min_role('admin'), ensure_tenant_access, and patch_settings functions. The PoC creates an unscoped HS256 JWT with roles=['admin'], sends X-Tenant-Id for victim tenant 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111, and uses a fake asyncpg connection modeling a DB membership row with role='viewer'. Output showed request.state.tenant_id=None, request.state.roles=['admin'], require_min_role accepted the JWT-only admin role, has_user_tenant_role SQL did not check role, and patch_settings returned an updated locale es-CO. I also ran a non-interactive bdb debugger trace. Breakpoint at app/core/security.py:179 showed token_tenant_id=None, x_tenant_id=victim, support_mode=False, roles=['admin']; breakpoint at app/api/v1/routes.py:84 showed request_tenant_id=None and tenant_id=victim immediately before the role-blind membership fallback. The previous commit diff also confirms this was introduced by removing the prior rejection: `if x_tenant_id and not token_tenant_id and not support_mode: ... X-Tenant-Id requires a tenant-scoped token`.

