# Attack-path analysis
Final: high | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: high | Policy adjusted: high
## Rationale
Keep as high. Static review and the supplied executable validation PoC both support the finding: require_min_role checks JWT roles only, has_user_tenant_role is role-blind, and privileged endpoints set app.tenant_id to the requested tenant before performing admin writes. This is a material cross-tenant privilege escalation and tenant-isolation break. It does not warrant critical based on the checked-in evidence because exploitation still requires a valid JWT with an admin role in some unscoped/non-victim context plus an existing membership row in the victim tenant, rather than being unauthenticated, universally exploitable, or full platform compromise.
## Likelihood
high - The vulnerable endpoints are normal remote API routes, and exploitation is straightforward once the attacker has a valid admin-capable unscoped/global JWT and any membership in the target tenant. Likelihood is not high because those identity/membership preconditions are more restrictive than an unauthenticated or ordinary low-privilege-only attack. | Remote network vector
## Impact
high - Successful exploitation crosses a tenant authorization boundary and grants admin-level writes in a victim tenant where the actor may only have viewer/agent membership. Demonstrated targets include tenant settings and WhatsApp channel configuration; similar tenant-admin routes can affect knowledge documents, prompts, and audit access.
## Assumptions
- The FastAPI core API is deployed for tenant users/admins similarly to the checked-in Dockerfile/docker-compose configuration.
- The identity provider can issue a user token whose roles claim contains admin without a tenant_id claim, or with roles not scoped to the requested victim tenant.
- An attacker can have any membership row, such as viewer or agent, in the target tenant and can learn that tenant UUID through normal product flows or prior access.
- Valid authenticated user JWT accepted by the API
- JWT roles claim satisfies require_min_role('admin')
- Token is unscoped to the victim tenant, or its admin role is not verified against the victim tenant
- Attacker auth_subject has any app.user_tenant_roles row for the victim tenant
- Attacker sends victim tenant_id in the path and/or X-Tenant-Id
## Path
User JWT(admin, no victim scope) + viewer membership in victim
  -> /v1/tenants/{victim}/settings
  -> require_min_role('admin') checks JWT roles only
  -> has_user_tenant_role checks existence only, not utr.role
  -> set_config('app.tenant_id', victim)
  -> victim tenant admin write
## Path evidence
- `app/main.py:21-25` - The vulnerable v1 API router is part of the main FastAPI application.
- `docker-compose.yml:19-21` - The API service is configured to listen on 0.0.0.0:8000 and publish port 8000 in the checked-in deployment configuration.
- `app/core/security.py:130-190` - authenticate_request accepts X-Tenant-Id input, preserves requested_tenant_id, only rejects mismatched X-Tenant-Id when token_tenant_id exists, stores JWT roles, and leaves request.state.tenant_id as token_tenant_id for non-support users.
- `app/core/security.py:218-233` - require_min_role authorizes based only on request.state.roles from the JWT; it does not resolve or verify a per-tenant role.
- `app/api/v1/routes.py:32-43` - tenant-admin and tenant-signup routers are protected by the JWT-only require_min_role('admin') dependency.
- `app/api/v1/routes.py:56-85` - has_user_tenant_role checks only existence of a user_tenant_roles row for the actor and tenant; ensure_tenant_access treats that as sufficient and does not check utr.role.
- `app/api/v1/routes.py:259-285` - PATCH /tenants/{tenant_id}/settings calls the weak ensure_tenant_access check, sets app.tenant_id to the requested tenant, and updates tenant settings.
- `app/api/v1/routes.py:288-307` - POST /tenants/{tenant_id}/channels/whatsapp uses the same access check and writes WhatsApp channel configuration for the requested tenant.
- `infra/postgres/01-schema.sql:88-97` - The user_tenant_roles table stores the tenant role, including lower roles such as viewer/agent, but the authorization fallback does not use this role column.
## Narrative
This is a real in-scope authorization flaw in the main runtime API. The code accepts an authenticated unscoped user token, keeps the JWT roles, and leaves request.state.tenant_id unset. Tenant-admin routers then require only require_min_role('admin'), which checks those JWT roles without tenant scope. The later tenant access fallback checks only whether the actor has any app.user_tenant_roles row for the target tenant and does not test utr.role. Privileged endpoints then set app.tenant_id to the path tenant and perform updates/inserts. The validation PoC exercised normal application functions and demonstrated a JWT with roles=['admin'] plus a viewer membership in the victim tenant updating tenant settings. Severity remains high because the impact is cross-tenant privilege escalation to admin-level writes, though exploitation requires a valid admin-capable JWT and some membership in the victim tenant, so it is not critical.
## Controls
- JWT signature, issuer, audience, expiry, and algorithm validation are present for user tokens.
- Router-level RBAC exists but is not tenant-scoped for this path.
- PostgreSQL RLS and app.tenant_id are present, but the endpoint sets app.tenant_id to the victim tenant after the flawed application-level authorization succeeds.
- Audit logging is present for modified tenant settings and channel upserts, but it is detective only and does not prevent exploitation.
- No checked-in test coverage was found that rejects low-privilege tenant membership combined with unscoped/global admin JWT roles.
## Blindspots
- Static-only review did not confirm the production ingress or whether port 8000 is internet-facing in the real deployment.
- Static-only review did not confirm real Auth0/custom-claim semantics, especially how often unscoped/global admin JWTs occur in production.
- No live database was queried, so the prevalence of multi-tenant users with low-privilege memberships is unknown.
- Only repository artifacts were reviewed; external IdP rules, API gateway policies, and production network controls could add constraints not visible here.